---
// Bare, Starlight-free page that hosts Redoc directly -- loaded inside an
// <iframe> by src/pages/api.astro, not visited on its own. Deliberately NOT
// wrapped in StarlightPage: Redoc expects to own its whole document's
// window/scroll (see api.astro's own header comment for the specifics that
// broke when it was embedded directly in the Starlight-framed page instead
// of a dedicated one), which a real, separate document guarantees in a way
// no amount of CSS on a shared page can.
//
// Reads which spec/theme to render from its own query string (?spec=...
// &theme=light|dark) rather than any inherited state, since as an iframe
// it has no other channel to the parent page.
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<meta charset="utf-8" />
		<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
		<meta name="robots" content="noindex" />
		<title>API Docs</title>
		{/* is:global -- this page's whole point is to host Redoc's own,
		   dynamically React-rendered DOM (#redoc-container's children,
		   .api-info-details, the react-tabs markup). Astro's default
		   per-component CSS scoping appends a generated class to every
		   selector segment (h5:where(.astro-xxxx)), which only elements
		   present in this file's own static template ever carry -- Redoc's
		   injected h5/summary/li elements never do, so a scoped rule
		   targeting them silently never matches (confirmed via computed
		   styles: the color rule below was present in the compiled CSS but
		   never applied). This is a single-purpose document with nothing
		   else to collide with, so going global is the actual fix rather
		   than a workaround. */}
		<style is:global>
			html,
			body {
				margin: 0;
				padding: 0;
				height: 100%;
			}
			#redoc-container {
				min-height: 100%;
			}
			/* Redoc's field-group labels ("Authorizations:", "Request Body
			   schema:", "Response Schema:", ...) use a hardcoded near-black
			   color (rgba(38, 50, 56, 0.7)) baked into their styled-
			   component, not derived from the theme object passed to
			   Redoc.init -- so it goes near-invisible against a dark
			   background unless overridden directly. api-viewer.js adds
			   .theme-dark to <html> when rendering the dark theme. */
			.theme-dark #redoc-container h5 {
				color: rgba(228, 230, 230, 0.85);
			}

			/* Same hardcoded-default-text-color issue as the h5 labels above,
			   on two more spots Redoc doesn't route through the theme object:
			   the request/response "media type" badge next to a schema
			   label (e.g. "application/json"), and the body text of Redoc's
			   own hover tooltips (e.g. "This is a versioned URL -- future
			   breaking changes ship as a new version..." on a path
			   variable). Both render solid/near-solid black regardless of
			   theme. Targeted by Redoc's own generated class names rather
			   than a generic tag selector -- neither element type is
			   otherwise identifiable, and both classes come from the
			   vendored, pinned Redoc build (public/vendor/redoc), so they
			   only change if that build is deliberately upgraded. */
			.theme-dark #redoc-container .sc-cWSHoV,
			.theme-dark #redoc-container [aria-hidden='true'] p {
				color: rgba(228, 230, 230, 0.85);
			}

			/* The HTTP-method badge (sidebar entries, and the endpoint's own
			   "POST /path" heading) is plain white text on the method's own
			   colors.http.* background -- fine against options' dark navy
			   (#0d5aa7), but every other method color in DARK_THEME is
			   light/saturated enough that white text fails WCAG AA against
			   it (get 2.78:1, post 2.01:1, delete 3.63:1, all below the
			   4.5:1 floor for this badge's small bold text -- measured via
			   the same relative-luminance formula WCAG uses). Dark text
			   passes comfortably against all of them (5:1-9:1) and is what
			   was asked for directly ("green bg pill... contrast color
			   text like navy or black"); the one exception is carved back
			   out to white since dark-on-navy is the one combination that
			   goes the other way (2.64:1). */
			.theme-dark .operation-type {
				color: #0e161b;
			}
			.theme-dark .operation-type.options {
				color: rgb(228, 230, 230);
			}

			/* The plain "Payload"/language tab above a request sample (no
			   http-status color of its own) uses the same light silver
			   swatch for its SELECTED background in every theme, relying on
			   the surrounding page's default dark text for contrast -- fine
			   on Redoc's light default, invisible once colors.text.primary
			   is overridden light for the rest of this dark theme (the
			   background stayed silver, the text became silver too).
			   Response-status tabs (tab-success/tab-error) aren't affected:
			   their selected text is colored from the response's own
			   semantic color (green/red), not text.primary, so they already
			   read fine and are excluded here. */
			.theme-dark .react-tabs__tab--selected:not(.tab-success):not(.tab-error) {
				color: #1c403b;
			}

			/* Collapsible wrapper api-viewer.js builds around the spec's long
			   info.description markdown -- see enhanceInfoSection() there. */
			.api-info-details {
				margin-block: 1em 0;
			}
			.api-info-details summary {
				cursor: pointer;
				font-weight: 600;
				padding-block: 0.25em;
				color: #11736b;
			}
			.theme-dark .api-info-details summary {
				color: #4fd1c5;
			}
		</style>
	</head>
	<body>
		<div id="redoc-container"></div>
		<script is:inline defer src="/vendor/redoc/redoc.standalone.js"></script>
		<script is:inline defer src="/assets/api-viewer.js"></script>
	</body>
</html>
